{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-86724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region  $D={(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(3,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-2,0)$, and $(2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/752c84f5ab0fb122102629e9ceada21c1bba0b8f0e93b3fffe0639e4284badab.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}-6(-2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}-6(2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the point $(-2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-86724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=16$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-11$ and ${f}_{max}=16$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=17$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=17$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=17$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+2y-1$ on the closed square $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1),$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & ={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =1-0+0-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & ={1}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1-1+2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}+2(1)-1 \\\\  & =0-1+2-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & ={0}^{3}-{0}^{2}+2(0)-1 \\\\  & =0-0+0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-167160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-86709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=7$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=7$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}-{x}^{2}-2{e}^{y}$ on the closed square $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/81b17231f65a3afcb9d1c65f2f67575f069eba981a033bd283d2d225f1d35bb1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =2(1){e}^{0}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2-1-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1){e}^{1}-(1)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e-1-2e \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,1) & =2(0){e}^{1}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =0-0-2e \\\\  & =-2e \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0){e}^{y}-(0)^{2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2e$ at the point $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $-1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-37369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2e"] ["math" "-e"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37369" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy-x}$ on the closed triangular region  $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices and the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/42b9bcda74e29cf7012fd9576ee7bcea92bfc3f58fab4c43c854892afe3acc44.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2{e}^{(0)(0)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2{e}^{(2)(0)-(2)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2} \\\\ f(0,1) & =2{e}^{(0)(1)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,2) & =2{e}^{(0)(2)-(0)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =2{e}^{(1/2)(3/2)-(1/2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[4]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2{e}^{-2}$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2\\sqrt[4]{e}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-86725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt[4]{e}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{e}"] ["math" "2{e}^{-2}"] ["math" "{e}^{-2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt[4]{e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,0)\\in D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives  \n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points: \n\n$$\n(1,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(-1,0),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,1) & =(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-1,0) & =(-1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,-1) & =(0)^{2}-(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and  $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and $(1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-83442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83442" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=2$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-4xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4y,\\,-4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-8$ | $10$ | $-8$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the points $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-86722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=10$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=8$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=10$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0)\\in D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(2,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+4 \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4 \\\\  & =13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (13-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)+4 \\\\  & =4+0-8+4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0-12+4 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-3)+4 \\\\  & =9+0+12+4 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $25$ at the point $(-3,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-37155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=0$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=5$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-5$ and ${f}_{max}=5$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=25$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=25$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=25$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\,2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-167165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=4$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-1$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(1,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-1 \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution $t=\\pi \\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi .$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1+0-2-1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(2)-1 \\\\  & =4+0-4-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2(-2)-1 \\\\  & =4+0+4-1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the point $(-2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-86723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=-1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-86723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=7$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=x-y+1$ on the closed rectangular region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,|x|\\le 1,\\,|y|\\le 1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it is never equal to zero. So, $f$ has no critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary: Notice that $D$ is the square with vertices at $(-1,-1)$, $(1,-1),$ $(1,1),$  and $(-1,1)$ \n\n- The line connecting $(-1,-1)$ and $(1,-1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,-1) \\\\  & =(t)-(-1)+1 \\\\  & =t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(1,-1)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =(1)-(t)+1 \\\\  & =2-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,1) \\\\  & =(t)-(1)+1 \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\nAnd finally, \n\n- The line connecting $(-1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨-1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (-1,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(-1,t) \\\\  & =(-1)-(t)+1 \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(1,1),\\,(-1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(1,-1)$ | $(1,1)$ | $(-1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(-1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3$ at the point $(1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-83474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-1,\\,x-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y-1=0 \\\\ x-1=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ is not in the interior of $D$,  we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the line $y=2-x,$ in the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t)-(0) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t⟨-2,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2-2t,2t) \\\\  & =(2-2t)(2t)-(2-2t)-(2t) \\\\  & =-4{t}^{2}+4t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-4{t}^{2}+4t-2)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -8t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(1,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0),\\,(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-83477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/5e38a1a4504c2e0f52fe383c948c52032d0bde6eda85d6621d51240cc88a4850.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9},$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2xy=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=R.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the entire segment of solutions ${(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,3]}.$ Since all these points do not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include them in the list of candidates for extrema (they lie on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider them in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(3,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)^{2}(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,3).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}(t) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,3).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(3,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}},t) \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}})}^{2}\\cdot t \\\\  & =9t-{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,3),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (9t-{t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 9-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(3-{t}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=\\sqrt{3},$ inside the interval $t\\in (0,3).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one  candidate point, $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(3,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the points $(0,0),\\,(3,0),(0,3),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $6\\sqrt{3}$ at the point $(\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-37162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=1$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=6\\sqrt{3}$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=6\\sqrt{3}$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=2$ and ${f}_{max}=6\\sqrt{3}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-37162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=6\\sqrt{3}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/8ce943f8a77fe5756fdaf77185d8b59493ef0da95da3605f1f2416786f9ec394.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=xy-4$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4},$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y,\\,x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0,\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4) \\\\  & =(t)(4)-4 \\\\  & =4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =(t)({t}^{2})-4 \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}-4)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(0,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(-2,4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(-2,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,4)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(2,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-83479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${f}_{min}=-10$ and ${f}_{max}=5$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=4$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=0$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=4$"] ["text" "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=12$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-83479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=4$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=x+y-xy$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-y & 1-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-y=0 \\\\ 1-x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =t+0-t(0) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t⟨1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,t) \\\\  & =t+t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2t-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (2t-{t}^{2})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2-2t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t=1$ does not belong to the range $t\\in (0,1),$ we do not include the corresponding point in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n- The line connecting $(1,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =1+t-t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total, we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4e4c73101039d535136f2af1fc3ed82bf0ac2029ad9de7987c63007c6498fa8a"} {"question" "q-167169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "q-167169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38dc84412be341cd2b41deb1dfa24ea7d9efcf9c772a54e2be0b026883f8395"} {"question" "e-12587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2-x-y$ on the closed region ${D}=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^2:\\,0\\leq x\\leq1,\\: x^2\\leq y\\leq 1 \\big\\}.$ \nYou are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0.5,-0.5),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(0.5,1),$ $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/example-12587.json" "file_sha256" "fec2dc075356aced91f957870f0fbf38d3aaa57ba590b3f79714b0d868cbc34c"} {"question" "e-12587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/a45fd135e45e9b40d06c217e9834923ea4f70809a26e020a8d3900e8fa0a0c22.png)\n\nSince the critical point $\\left(0.5,-0.5\\right)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.   \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n\\left(0.5,1\\right), \\quad (0,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.5,1) & ={(0.5)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-0.5-1 \\\\  & =0.25-2.5 \\\\  & =-2.25 \\\\ f(0,0) & ={0}^{2}-{0}^{2}-0-0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}-{1}^{2}-0-1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1,1) & ={1}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2.25$ at the point $\\left(0.5,1\\right),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/example-12587.json" "file_sha256" "fec2dc075356aced91f957870f0fbf38d3aaa57ba590b3f79714b0d868cbc34c"} {"question" "e-12588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the global extrema of the function  $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2-4x$ on the closed region $D=\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4 \\big\\}.$   You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0) \\in D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/example-12588.json" "file_sha256" "b91bf7ac0e1881cabd8a03872edfaa37b700c0df7b0efb054c9d7eb76d8ed892"} {"question" "e-12588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince the point $( 2 , 0 )$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step). \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2\\cos{t}, 2\\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution, $t = \\pi \\in (0,2\\pi).$ But we also have to check the endpoint of the interval, $t=0.$ So, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0), \\quad  (-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2) \\\\  & =4+0-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2) \\\\  & =4+0+8 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-4$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(-2,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/example-12588.json" "file_sha256" "b91bf7ac0e1881cabd8a03872edfaa37b700c0df7b0efb054c9d7eb76d8ed892"} {"question" "e-12589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/0ce5cde24ada296d945726d9f8e2f0b85900e62e9891f7ba4e25b6c181b076aa.png)\n\n   \n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2-2x+y^2+3$ on the closed region\n${D} = \\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\: : \\: x \\geq 0,\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq4 \\},$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/example-12589.json" "file_sha256" "63d2050f3e587801a5afef43408998b166e9341bb026fc86fd31b1f666b4c632"} {"question" "e-12589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol{f}' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/assets/815ebf6434a4b776ff59fb59e5b0783162e9ee69c091ae6d0f59ca94e8cf771b.png)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,0).$ Since the point $(1,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 0, t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-2(0)+(t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is inside the interval $t \\in (-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on this boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(0,0).$\n\n- The half-circle connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}},t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2}-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =7-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (7-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives the solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in(-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of  $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(2,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain a total of four candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (0,-2), \\quad (2,0), \\quad (0,2).\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $7$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(0,-2),$  and $(0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075482/example-12589.json" "file_sha256" "63d2050f3e587801a5afef43408998b166e9341bb026fc86fd31b1f666b4c632"}] "usage" {"e-12589" [] "q-37155" [] "e-12588" [] "q-37162" [] "q-37369" [] "e-12587" []} "basis" 2754 "retractions" [[17592186124979 :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x, y) = 2xe^{y} - x^{2} - 2e^{y}$ on the closed square $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1}$. You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1, 0)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices."] [17592186124951 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2} - 4x + 4$ on the closed region $D = {(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 9}$. You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2, 0) ∈ D$."] [17592186124953 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1947/Source/Images/q-37162.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2}y$ on the closed region $D = {(x, y): x \\ge 0, y \\ge 0, x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 9}$, shown above."] [17592186124137 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2-x-y$ on the closed region ${D}=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^2:\\,0\\leq x\\leq1,\\: x^2\\leq y\\leq 1 \\big\\}$.\nYou are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0.5,-0.5)$, and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(0.5,1)$, $(0,0)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,1)$."] [17592186124137 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D$, the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D$.\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D$.\n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1947/Source/Images/e66cba0a9ab0e53f06f149f0e9f15d00.png)\n\nSince the critical point\n\n$$\n\\left(0.5,-0.5\\right)\n$$\n\ndoes not lie inside $D$, we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f$. These are\n\n$$\n\\left(0.5,1\\right), \\quad (0,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0.5, 1) &= (0.5)^{2} - 1^{2} - 0.5 - 1 \\\\\n&= 0.25 - 2.5 \\\\\n&=-2.25 \\\\\nf(0, 0) &= 0^{2} - 0^{2} - 0 - 0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\\\\nf(0, 1) &= 0^{2} - 1^{2} - 0 - 1 \\\\\n&=-2 \\\\\nf(1, 1) &= 1^{2} - 1^{2} - 1 - 1 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D$, we have that\n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2.25$ at the point $\\left(0.5,1\\right)$, and\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0)$."] [17592186124138 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2-4x$ on the closed region $D=\\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4 \\big\\}$. You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0) \\in D$."] [17592186124138 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D$, the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D$.\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D$.\n\nSince the point $(2, 0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D$, so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 =4\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$[MATH: \\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2\\cos{t}, 2\\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).]$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t) &= f(x(t), y(t)) \\\\\n&= f(2\\cos t, 2\\sin t) \\\\\n&= (2\\cos t)^{2} + (2\\sin t)^{2} - 4(2\\cos t) \\\\\n&= 4\\cos^{2} t + 4\\sin^{2} t - 8\\cos t \\\\\n&= 4(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t) - 8\\cos t \\\\\n&= 4 - 8\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi)$, we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF^{′}(t) &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - 8\\cos t)^{′} &= 0 \\\\\n8\\sin t &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin t &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution,\n\n$$\nt = \\pi \\in (0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nBut we also have to check the endpoint of the interval, $t=0$. So, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi$. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0), \\quad (-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2, 0) &= (2)^{2} + (0)^{2} - 4(2) \\\\\n&= 4 + 0 - 8 \\\\\n&=-4 \\\\\nf(-2, 0) &= (-2)^{2} + (0)^{2} - 4(-2) \\\\\n&= 4 + 0 + 8 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D$, we have that\n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-4$ at the point $(2,0)$, and\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(-2,0)$."] [17592186124139 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2-2x+y^2+3$ on the closed region\n${D} = \\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\:: \\: x \\geq 0,\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq4 \\}$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1947/Source/Images/34d78e27d5c4b6ec66a4eb4b11e0a7e6.png)"] [17592186124139 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D$, the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D$.\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region.\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}' = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n[2x - 2, 2y]\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D$, and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n{2x - 2 = 0; 2y = 0⟹{x = 1; y = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,0)$. Since the point $(1,0)$ is in the interior of $D$, we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 0, t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-2,2)$.\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n$F(t)|= f(x(t), y(t)); = f(0, t); = (0)^{2} - 2(0) + (t)^{2} + 3; = t^{2} + 3$.\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2)$, we solve the following equation:\n$F^{′}(t)|= 0; (t^{2} + 3)^{′}|= 0; 2t|= 0; t|= 0$\nThe solution $t=0$ is inside the interval $t \\in (-2,2)$. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on this boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(0,0)$.\n\n- The half-circle connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n$r|= ⟨\\sqrt{4 - t^{2}}, t⟩, t ∈ (-2, 2)$.\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n$F(t)|= f(x(t), y(t)); = f(\\sqrt{4 - t^{2}}, t); = (\\sqrt{4 - t^{2}})^{2} - 2\\sqrt{4 - t^{2}} + t^{2} + 3; = 7 - 2\\sqrt{4 - t^{2}}$.\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2)$, we solve the following equation:\n$F^{′}(t)|= 0; (7 - 2\\sqrt{4 - t^{2}})^{′}|= 0; \\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{4 - t^{2}}}|= 0; t|= 0$\nThe final equation gives the solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in(-2,2)$. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(2,0)$.\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain a total of four candidates on the boundary:\n$(0,0), \\quad (0,-2), \\quad (2,0), \\quad (0,2)$.\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $7$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D$, we have that\n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,0)$, and\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(0,-2)$, and $(0,2)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122729 17592186122730 17592186122731 17592186122732 17592186122733 17592186122741 17592186122742 17592186122743 17592186122744 17592186122745 17592186122897 17592186122898 17592186122899 17592186122900 17592186122901] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122728 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122728 :answer-field/id] [17592186122728 :answer-field/key] [17592186122728 :answer-field/type] [17592186122740 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122740 :answer-field/id] [17592186122740 :answer-field/key] [17592186122740 :answer-field/type] [17592186122896 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122896 :answer-field/id] [17592186122896 :answer-field/key] [17592186122896 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
